Star and Snowflake schemas are the two most common ways to logically organize a dimensional data model in a data warehouse.
The core difference boils down to how they handle dimension tables: Star schemas keep dimensions fully denormalized for query speed,
while Snowflake schemas normalize those dimensions to save storage space and enforce strict data integrity.

Technical breakdown :-
Both schemas revolve around a central Fact Table (which stores quantitative metrics like 'sales amount') 
connected to surrounding Dimension Tables (which store descriptive attributes like 'customer', 'product', or 'date')

Star Schema (Denormalized):
Every dimension connects directly to the central fact table,creating a shape that looks like a star.
A dimension like dim_product will contain the product name, its category, and its subcategory all in the same row.

Pros: Extremely fast read performance because queries require very few JOINs. The logic is simple and intuitive for BI analysts to understand.

Cons: Storage redundancy. If 1,000 products belong to the 'Electronics' category, the string 'Electronics' is repeated 1,000 times, 
which can lead to data anomalies if an update isn't handled carefully.

Snowflake Schema (Normalized):
Dimension tables are broken down further into sub-dimensions, creating a branching 'snowflake' shape.
The dim_product table connects to the fact table, but it will have a foreign key pointing to a separate dim_subcategory table, which in turn points to a dim_category table.

Pros: Highly efficient storage with zero redundancy (3NF or partial 3NF). It enforces strict data integrity because updating a category name happens in exactly one place.

Cons: Slower read performance. A BI query trying to get sales by category now requires multiple expensive JOINs to traverse the branches back to the fact table.
It is also much harder for analysts to query directly."

Buisness Context :-
The choice is a classic trade-off between compute and storage.
In the 1990s, when disk storage was astronomically expensive, Snowflake schemas were highly popular to save space.
Today, storage in the cloud (like S3) is incredibly cheap, but compute (like Snowflake credits or BigQuery slots) is expensive.
Therefore, we generally optimize for fast reads and fewer joins.

Design Decision :-
When I architect a modern data warehouse today—especially Kimball-style architectures in the Gold layer of a Lakehouse—I almost exclusively default to the Star Schema.
The minor cost of duplicated storage is vastly outweighed by the massive performance gains and simplicity it provides to downstream BI tools like Looker or Tableau.
I only normalize into a Snowflake pattern if I am dealing with an extremely complex, slowly changing dimension (SCD) where the redundancy becomes mathematically unmanageable.
